          "passageTitle": "Command of Evidence - Weaken — Q7",
          "passage": "Rafael Núñez and colleagues studied how\nmembers of the Yupno, an Indigenous group in\nPapua New Guinea, conceptualize time. The\nresearchers recorded Yupno speakers explaining\ncertain temporal words and phrases, such as\nabjuk, a present-oriented expression that\ntranslates to \"now,\" and coded each speaker's\nmanual gestures. Previous research has found a\ntendency in many cultures to make temporal\ndistinctions using spatial concepts and gestures,\nparticularly along egocentric axes (i.e., relative\nto the orientation of the speaker): for instance,\nEnglish speakers often refer to the front/back\naxis to describe events in time. In an\nanthropology paper, a student claims that the\ntendency toward ego-based conceptualizations of\ntime is universal.",
